---
<QuestionLayout
  title="Can I build a mobile app with Traverse?"
  description="Partially. iOS now has a production, certified runtime bridge. Android and .NET/WinUI embedders are still working code in progress with no public release. Here's the exact status, honestly."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/can-traverse-build-mobile-apps.html"
  category="Question"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>Partially — and the status is no longer the same across both major mobile OSes, so it's worth being precise about which platform you mean. Traverse's mature, shipped targets are native (Windows, macOS, Linux), the browser, and now iOS. Android and .NET/WinUI are still genuinely in progress, not yet publicly released.</p>

  <h2>iOS — a production runtime bridge, certified</h2>
  <p><code>packages/swift/TraverseEmbedder</code> now ships <code>WasmiHostBridgeClient</code> as its production runtime loader: it embeds the <code>wasmi</code> interpreter behind a narrow, audited C ABI, packaged as the <code>TraverseSwiftHost</code> XCFramework, and enforces host-owned memory and fuel limits on every call. This is the certification path defined by Spec 074 (Swift Native Resource-Control Certification) and governed by ADR-0014 (selecting <code>wasmi</code> for the Apple runtime profile) and ADR-0015 (the production C-ABI boundary). It replaced the earlier WasmKit-based bridge, which is now deprecated and kept only for source compatibility. The original feasibility spike that unblocked this — <a href="/blog/native-ios-runtime-foundation.html">written up here</a> — is history at this point, not the current state.</p>

  <h2>Android — in progress, unreleased</h2>
  <p><code>packages/kotlin/TraverseEmbedder</code> has a working <code>ChicoryRuntimeBridge</code> pinned to a specific Chicory version. Bundle validation and lifecycle types are in place. What's missing is Compose reference-app integration, which is tracked by its own open issue, and a public release. If you want to help move it forward, that issue is the place to look.</p>

  <h2>.NET / WinUI — in progress, unreleased</h2>
  <p>The same shape as Android: <code>packages/dotnet/TraverseEmbedder</code> has a working Wasmtime .NET-backed bridge. Request marshalling, event subscriptions, evidence publication, and the shared conformance suite are still open work.</p>

  <p>If your app's core problem is mobile UI, Traverse isn't a full toolkit for that yet on either platform. If you're building an iOS app today, the runtime bridge is real and certified; Android and .NET/WinUI still need a public release before you'd want to depend on them. The contract you write now doesn't change when those embedders ship. Track live status on the <a href="/platforms.html">Platforms page</a> — it's the same page we link internally, not a separate marketing version.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
